
<% content_for :title, "Depression Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-depression">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / DEPRESSION
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WHEN</span>
        <span>EVERYTHING</span>
        <span>FEELS HEAVY.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR THE LOW DAYS,
        LONG WEEKS, AND MONTHS THAT DRAG ON.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT THE LOW<br>
        LOOKS LIKE.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Bipolar depression isn't always a few days
          of feeling sad. For you, it can last months.
          Two turns into three, then three turns into four.
          Four months or longer of exhaustion,
          disconnection, and struggling to find
          interest in things that usually matter.
          Life keeps happening while your
          ability to participate in it shrinks.
        </p>

        <p>
          Everything requires more effort.
          Getting out of bed, answering messages,
          making food, cleaning the house,
          showering, or dealing
          with ordinary responsibilities can
          feel disproportionately difficult.
          Even things you enjoy may stop
          feeling enjoyable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol can look appealing because
          you've used it to change how you feel.
          Sometimes drinking made you happy
          when you were sad. Sometimes it
          gave you something to look forward
          to when nothing else sounded interesting.
        </p>

        <p>
          When depression stretches on,
          the idea of feeling different for
          a few hours can become persuasive.
          You may not even be looking for
          happiness. You might just want
          relief from being yourself
          in that particular moment.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to deny that alcohol
          once felt good. But temporary relief
          isn't the same as treatment.
          Drinking can interfere with sleep,
          worsen depressive symptoms, impair
          judgment, and create problems
          that remain after the alcohol wears off.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You can want a break from depression
            without returning to something
            that makes your life harder.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR WARNING SIGNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT STARTS<br>
        SLIPPING?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- THOUGHTS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR THOUGHTS</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention when your thinking
            becomes increasingly negative,
            hopeless, or absolute.
            Thoughts like "Nothing matters,"
            "This is never going to end,"
            or "What's the point?"
            can start feeling like facts.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may replay mistakes,
            assume people don't want
            to hear from you, or
            convince yourself that
            you're falling behind
            everyone else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for the moment
            those thoughts begin
            making a case for drinking:
            "I already feel terrible,"
            "One night won't matter,"
            or "At least I'd feel
            something different."
          </p>


<p>
  Suicidal ideation is also part of your
  depressive episodes. When depression
  becomes severe, thoughts of death
  or suicide can enter the picture.
  These thoughts are important warning
  signs, even when you have no intention
  of acting on them.
</p>

<p>
  Don't dismiss suicidal thoughts
  because you've experienced them before.
  Notice when they begin, how persistent
  they become, and whether their intensity
  changes. Tell your therapist
  when they appear rather than waiting
  for them to become overwhelming. Do not drink them away. 
  They will return stronger and will not go away.
</p>


          <p>
            Depression can make the
            future look permanently
            bleak. Recognize that
            your current outlook
            may be shaped by
            the episode itself.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- DAILY LIFE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR DAILY LIFE</h3>

          <p>
            Notice when you're spending
            more time in bed, sleeping
            much more or less than usual,
            ignoring messages, or
            withdrawing from people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meals become irregular.
            Basic hygiene starts
            slipping. Projects sit
            untouched. Things that
            normally interest you
            feel like obligations.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may stop making plans
            because you don't expect
            to enjoy them, or cancel
            things because the effort
            feels impossible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to changes
            from your usual baseline,
            especially when several
            appear together and persist.
          </p>

          <p>
            These changes are information,
            not evidence that you've
            failed. They tell you
            it's time to adjust your
            expectations and involve
            your psych and therapist.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / THE PROMISE OF RELIEF -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / THE PROMISE OF RELIEF
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO YOU WANT<br>
        TO FEEL DIFFERENT?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- THE PROMISE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU'RE LOOKING FOR</h3>

          <p>
            You might want happiness,
            excitement, comfort, or
            the ability to stop
            thinking for a while.
            You might want an
            ordinary evening to
            feel less empty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alcohol can seem like
            a shortcut because you
            remember the immediate
            change it created.
            The anticipation alone
            might feel like something
            to look forward to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask yourself what you're
            actually hoping to change.
            Do you want connection?
            Relief from sadness?
            A distraction from
            hopeless thoughts?
            Something pleasurable?
          </p>

          <p>
            The need is worth
            understanding.
            The suggested solution
            still deserves scrutiny.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- THE REALITY -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOUR EXPERIENCE SAYS</h3>

          <p>
            Drinking has made you
            feel better temporarily.
            That's part of why
            you kept returning to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            But the relief didn't
            last. Alcohol didn't
            resolve the depression,
            and it brought consequences
            that lasted well beyond
            the evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Play the tape forward.
            Remember the anticipation,
            the first drink, and
            the feeling you wanted.
            Then keep going.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider what happened
            when the effects wore off.
            What did you wake up to?
            What happened to your
            responsibilities,
            relationships, sobriety,
            and trust in yourself?
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Remember the relief.
              Remember the rest.
              Both belong in
              the same story.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / A SMALLER PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / A SMALLER PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CAN YOU<br>
        MANAGE TODAY?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- NEXT SMALL STEP -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR NEXT SMALL STEP</h3>

          <p>
            When you're depressed,
            don't build a plan
            for the version of
            yourself who has plenty
            of energy and motivation.
            Work with the capacity
            you actually have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with one thing:
            take your prescribed
            medication, drink water,
            eat something, wash your
            face, or change clothes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can do more,
            choose another small task.
            Sit somewhere different,
            listen to music, spend
            time with Banjo, or
            work on something creative
            for a few minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have to
            turn the day around.
            You only need to
            decide what is
            manageable next.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              A smaller plan isn't
              a lesser plan.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- CONNECTION -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>CONNECTION WITHOUT THE PERFORMANCE</h3>

          <p>
            Depression can make
            conversation feel exhausting.
            You might not want
            to explain yourself,
            answer questions,
            or pretend you're
            having a good day.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can still reach out
            without putting on
            a performance.
            Send a short message
            to someone you trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm having a rough
            depression day. I don't
            really feel like talking,
            but I wanted to
            check in."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm struggling today.
            Could you keep me
            company for a little
            while? We don't have
            to do anything."
          </p>

          <p>
            You can ask someone
            to call, send a message,
            visit, or help with
            something practical.
            Connection doesn't
            have to require
            a lot of energy.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- BACKUP -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR BACKUP</h3>

          <p>
            If your first idea
            feels impossible,
            make it smaller.
            If you can't shower,
            wash your face.
            If you can't cook,
            eat something simple.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can't work
            on a project,
            look at something
            you've already made.
            If you can't
            manage a conversation,
            send one short text.
          </p>

          <p>
            If someone doesn't
            answer, contact
            another trusted person,
            someone from Harmony,
            or your therapist.
          </p>

          <p>
            If drinking starts
            sounding appealing,
            don't buy alcohol
            or make plans that
            involve it. Put some
            distance between
            the craving and
            the opportunity.
          </p>

          <p>
            A backup plan isn't
            supposed to be
            impressive. It's
            supposed to be usable
            when you're struggling.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- CARE & SAFETY -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR CARE &amp; SAFETY PLAN</h3>

          <p>
            Bipolar depression can
            become severe and
            last a long time.
            Don't wait until
            you've spent months
            struggling before
            letting your psych and therapist know what's happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Continue taking your
            medication as prescribed,
            track changes in mood
            and sleep, and contact
            your psych
            when your symptoms
            worsen or your
            functioning declines.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're thinking
            seriously about drinking,
            contact a trusted friend.
            Tell them directly
            what's happening.
          </p>

         
<p>
  Because suicidal ideation can accompany
  your depressive episodes, it needs
  to be part of your safety plan
  from the beginning, not only
  when you're in crisis.
</p>

<p>
  When suicidal thoughts appear,
  tell your psych or therapist and someone you trust.
  Follow your established safety plan and
  avoid alcohol.
</p>



          <p>
            <strong>
              When depression becomes
              too much to manage
              with your usual plan,
              getting more support
              is the next step.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>
